<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Toba–Fushimi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Toba-Fushimi · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Toba–Fushimi, fought from 27 to 31 January 1868 south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, including troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aizu">Aizu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuwana">Kuwana</a>, were defeated by the smaller but better-armed armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and Chōshū fighting under the imperial banner. The former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a> fled to Edo, and many domains went over to the new imperial government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-toba-fushimi.jpg" alt="Battle of Toba–Fushimi" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo memories 1868 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_of_Meijiishin_Fushimi_no_senseki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It began with the defeat of the shogunate's forces at <b>Toba-Fushimi</b>, continued with the negotiated surrender of Edo and ended with the fall of the last Tokugawa loyalists at Hakodate, which brought shogunal military power to an end.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Kuwana troops fought at <b>Toba-Fushimi</b> in 1868, and Sadaaki continued the struggle in the north and Hakodate while the domain surrendered.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuwana-domain">Kuwana Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>After the shogunate's defeat at <b>Toba-Fushimi</b>, the new government ordered northern domains to punish Aizu and Shonai.</blockquote><a href="/state/ouetsu-reppan-domei">Ouetsu Reppan Domei</a></li><li><blockquote>Takamatsu fought for the shogunate at <b>Toba-Fushimi</b> in 1868 and was occupied and punished by the new government.</blockquote><a href="/state/takamatsu-domain">Takamatsu Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>At the battle of <b>Toba-Fushimi</b> in 1868 Tsu troops, stationed to block the imperial forces, instead turned their guns on the shogunate's army, contributing to its defeat.</blockquote><a href="/state/tsu-domain">Tsu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
